course: dictionary
lesson: 10306
title: Many meanings of tout
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «tout» has more than one meaning, so you must use the context to find the right one. Read the words around it, then ask which meaning fits.
explain: To tout means to praise something loudly so that people will want it or believe it. In news, a product is «touted as» the best. The advert shouts about it, and you should ask whether the praise is true.
explain: In Britain, a tout is a person who buys tickets for concerts or matches and sells them at a higher price. «Tickets should come from the official seller, not a tout.» Here the sentence tells you a tout is someone to avoid.
explain: In French, «tout» means «all» or «everything». Look at «Ne mangez pas tout le chocolat!», which means don't eat all the chocolate. The word ne ... pas makes it a "don't", and tout means all.
fact: To tout means to praise something loudly so people will buy it or believe it.
fact: A tout (UK) is a person who buys tickets and sells them at a higher price.
fact: In America, a ticket tout is called a scalper.
fact: In French, «tout» means «all» or «everything», as in «tout le monde» (everybody).
fact: «Tout droit» means straight on, and it sounds like «too DRWA»; «à droite» means on the right and ends with a t sound.
exercise: «The new phone was touted as the best ever made.» What does touted mean here? | Measured very carefully | *Praised loudly so people want it | Dropped on the floor by mistake | Hidden away from everyone
exercise: What does a ticket tout do? | Checks tickets at the gate | Designs posters for concerts | Gives free tickets to school children | *Buys tickets and sells them at a higher price
exercise: In «Ne mangez pas tout le chocolat!», what does tout mean? | Straight on | *All | Nothing at all | Sweet
test: In «tickets should come from the official seller, not a tout», what is a tout? | An official ticket office | A type of music | A football player | *A person who buys tickets and resells them at a higher price
test: Which phrase ends with a t sound? | *à droite | tout droit | Neither of them | Both of them
exam: In French, what does «tout le monde» mean? | A ticket tout | *Everybody | Straight on | Nobody at all
